# AI Agent Customizations

This is a TurboGears project. Use Gearbox as the entrypoint for TurboGears development commands.

## Project setup

Use the project-local environment for every command. Run `uv sync --extra development`
before the first `uv run` so the project environment is fully provisioned.

`development.ini` is for local development; `test.ini` is for tests and references
the development configuration.

## Inspection before guessing

Do not guess TurboGears APIs, predicates, or dispatch semantics. Before importing
an unfamiliar framework symbol, run `uv run gearbox tgdocs`, then search only the
cache path it prints. If the documentation is insufficient, report the gap
instead of inspecting installed package internals or searching outside the project.

Inspect the project with compact read-only commands before reading files:

    uv run gearbox tginfo summary --project . --config development.ini
    uv run gearbox tginfo routes --project . --config development.ini
    uv run python -m pytest --collect-only -q

Do not dump whole packages with `find`/`sed` or read every file up front. Use
`tginfo` to locate the specific file, then read only that file.

## Project structure

- `{{package}}/controllers/root.py` — RootController: HTML pages, login/logout, mounts the API controller at `/api`
- `{{package}}/controllers/api/__init__.py` — APIController: REST sub-controllers mounted under `/api`, OpenAPI schema and docs endpoints
- `{{package}}/controllers/api/<resource>.py` — REST endpoints for a resource (RestController); create with `gearbox scaffold`
- `{{package}}/model/__init__.py` — DBSession, DeclarativeBase, model exports
- `{{package}}/model/auth.py` — User, Group, Permission (repoze.who auth models)
- `{{package}}/model/<resource>.py` — your resource model; create with `gearbox scaffold model`
- `{{package}}/config/app_cfg.py` — application configurator: renderers, auth, database
- `{{package}}/config/application.py` — WSGI app factory
- `{{package}}/websetup/bootstrap.py` — seed data (runs via `gearbox setup-app`)
- `{{package}}/websetup/schema.py` — schema creation (runs via `gearbox setup-app`)
- `{{package}}/templates/` — Kajiki XHTML templates; `login.xhtml` for the login page, `api_docs.xhtml` for API docs
- `{{package}}/public/` — static files served from the application root
- `{{package}}/tests/__init__.py` — TestController base and `setup_database()` helper
- `{{package}}/tests/functional/` — WebTest functional tests
- `development.ini` — development config (database URL, auth, debug)
- `test.ini` — test config (in-memory database, references development.ini)
{{if sqlalchemy and migrations}}- `migration/` — Alembic migrations{{endif}}

## Skills

Quickstart installs TurboGears skills into ``.agents/skills/``. Read only the
skill needed for the current operation; do not preload all skills.

- **tg-inspect**: discover routes, controllers, models, templates, or project facts
- **tg-scaffold**: create conventional models, controllers, templates, or tests
- **tg-shell**: run Python in the loaded application context for runtime checks

For runtime debugging, use `uv run gearbox tgshell -c test.ini` with WebTest requests.

## Other commands

Use `--json` only when a script must parse the result.
Static files under `{{package}}/public/` are served from the application root;
for example, `public/app.js` is available at `/app.js`.

{{if sqlalchemy}}
For SQLAlchemy WebTest fixtures, use `test.ini`, commit setup data with
`transaction.commit()`, and call `model.DBSession.remove()` between tests.
{{endif}}
Use `uv run gearbox scaffold` for conventional new files.

{{if sqlalchemy and migrations}}
Migration commands live in README.rst under "Database Migrations".
Do not run `setup-app`, migration upgrades, or other database-mutating commands
as routine inspection; `uv run gearbox migrate -c development.ini db_version` is the only read-only check.
{{else}}
Do not run `setup-app` or other database-mutating commands as routine inspection.
{{endif}}
`tginfo` and `tgshell` load application code, so project-defined startup code
can execute.
